  "notes": "West leads the ♠10 against 6♥. West's spade lead should be ducked in dummy and ruffed by South. South should cash 2 top hearts. When West discards on the second round, South must play carefully in case West has 4 diamonds.\n\nThe order of plays must be precise. South must cash the ♦A and ♦K. If East started with a singleton diamond, he cannot afford to ruff or he will endplay himself (East is marked with the ♠A and ♣K for his opening bid).\n\nEast will discard and a third round of diamonds is played. Again East must pitch. A spade is played from North and ruffed by South. South plays a diamond and discards North's last spade. Once again East must discard to avoid being endplayed. However, if West holds the ♣J, he is now endplayed. A club return will pick up that suit. A spade return will concede a ruff and discard.",
